The '''2030 AFA World Cup''' is scheduled to be the 19th [[AFA World Cup]], the quadrennial international football tournament for men's senior national teams organised by the [[Adonian Football Association]] (AFA), a body of the [[Adonian Community]]. The tournament is scheduled to be held in [[Dagit]] from June to July 2030. However, the hosting arrangement is under threat following an [[Adonian Community]] resolution that could strip Dagit of hosting rights if Senator [[Ronaldo Batot]] is not surrendered to the [[Adonian Court of Justice]] (ACJ).

== Host selection ==

Four nations submitted bids to host the 2030 AFA World Cup: [[Dagit]], [[Orma]], [[Chatar]], and [[Mercia]]. [[Asikyira]], which had been widely expected to bid, was ineligible under AFA rules as an Abyalan confederation member following the 2026 World Cup co-hosted by Balisca and Sanrobonia. The 210-member AFA convened in [[Brukelyn]], [[United Midlands]], on 12 March 2022 to vote to select the hosts.

Dagit's bid, led by the Dagiti Football Federation (DFF) and backed personally by President [[Enrique Magalona]], emphasised the country's large and passionate football culture, its growing infrastructure, and the symbolic power of bringing the World Cup to North Fosia for the first time since the [[2010 AFA World Cup|2010 tournament]] in [[Nusaraya]]. The bid proposed twelve venues across the archipelago, with the final to be held at the [[Akyatan National Stadium]] (capacity 75,000). The bid received strong support from the [[Adonian Community]], which viewed the tournament as an opportunity to showcase Dagit's democratic development and economic modernisation following the [[Dagiti Revolution of 2009]].

Orma's bid, led by the Orman Football Federation, emphasised the country's status as the highest-ranked AFA team never to have hosted the tournament, its Mesogean infrastructure, its experience hosting regional tournaments, and its position as a bridge between Kaftia and Illypnia. Chatar's bid focused on its growing football culture and strategic position between Kaftia and Fosia, while Mercia's bid highlighted its Illypnian infrastructure and commercial appeal.

The geopolitical dimension heavily influenced the vote. The North Fosian and East Fosian blocs voted strongly in favour of Dagit, viewing it as their region's turn to host. The Mesogean and Kaftian associations split between Orma and Chatar, while the Lurandian bloc leaned toward Dagit. Mercia, lacking a natural regional bloc, struggled to build a coalition beyond its Illypnian allies.

On the first round of balloting, Dagit led with 88 votes, followed by Orma with 62, Chatar with 38, and Mercia with 22. As Mercia was eliminated, its votes redistributed primarily to Orma on the second round. On the second round, Dagit secured 112 votes to Orma's 98, achieving the required majority of 106.

