== Hosting crisis ==

{{main|Ronaldo Batot}}

The hosting arrangement has been thrown into crisis by the [[Ronaldo Batot]] affair. Batot, a Dagiti senator and former chief of the [[Dagiti National Police]], is wanted by the [[Adonian Court of Justice]] (ACJ) for crimes against humanity stemming from his role as the "chief enforcer" of the anti-narcotics campaign under former President [[Rodrigo Tañada]], which resulted in over 12,000 deaths. Tañada himself was arrested in March 2025 and is currently detained at the ACJ facility in [[International City of Areza|Areza]].

On 11 May 2026, the ACJ unsealed a confidential arrest warrant for Batot. When Bureau of Primary Investigation (BPI) agents attempted to serve the warrant inside the [[Kongreso sang Dagit]] building, Batot fled through the hallways and was placed in protective custody by the Senate leadership. He escaped the Kongreso premises in the early hours of 14 May 2026 and remains a fugitive.

=== Adonian Community resolution ===

On 28 May 2026, the [[Adonian Community Assembly]] passed Resolution AC/2026/447 by a vote of 127–31–22, conditioning Dagit's hosting of the 2030 AFA World Cup on the surrender of Ronaldo Batot to the ACJ. The resolution stated that a member state "harbouring a fugitive wanted by the Adonian Court of Justice for crimes against humanity" was incompatible with the principles underpinning the AFA World Cup, and that the AC would recommend to the AFA Executive Committee that hosting rights be withdrawn if Batot was not transferred to ACJ custody within 180 days.

The resolution was sponsored by [[Balisca]], [[Burawa]], and [[Volisania]], and was supported by the [[Adonian Commission on Human Rights]], which had documented systematic patterns of extrajudicial executions during Batot's tenure as DNP Chief. Opponents of the resolution, primarily from East Fosian and North Fosian delegations, argued that the AC was inappropriately politicising sport and that the hosting decision was a matter for the AFA, not the Adonian Community.

=== Magalona administration response ===

President [[Enrique Magalona]], already under investigation for ₱10 billion in corruption related to the Yanson family's business interests, has faced mounting pressure to either cooperate with the ACJ arrest warrant or protect Batot — a key political ally whose Senate votes have been instrumental in shielding the Magalona administration from legislative scrutiny. Magalona's spokesperson stated that the administration would "respect due process" while noting that the ACJ warrant required "proper domestic legal channels" before enforcement.

On 21 May 2026, the Dagiti Supreme Court voted 9–5–1 to deny Batot's petition for a temporary restraining order, clearing the way for law enforcement to execute the arrest. However, the government has been accused of deliberately slow-walking the enforcement, with opposition lawmakers alleging that Magalona's reluctance stems from Batot's knowledge of the Kizan Systems contract irregularities and the potential exposure of the Yanson family's involvement in diverted funds.

=== AFA response ===

The Adonian Football Association has stated that it is "monitoring the situation closely" and that it retains the right to withdraw hosting rights in accordance with its hosting agreements. AFA President [[Kiyokawa Tadashige]] said in a statement on 2 June 2026 that the organisation "remains committed to delivering a successful World Cup in Dagit" but that "the integrity of the tournament and the principles it represents must be upheld." The AFA has reportedly begun contingency planning for an alternative host, with [[Orma]] — the runner-up in the original bidding process — considered the most likely replacement.

== Qualification ==

The qualification process is scheduled to begin in March 2027. The regional allocation of the 36 final tournament places is expected to be confirmed by the AFA Executive Committee in late 2026.

